From f5ed2090eedc233c7ba9d567c0e59fa4d3e0655f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andreas Ekdahl <1434571+andtii@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 17:00:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] simd: AES round instructions on u8x16 (AES-NI, ARMv8 AES)
 with checks and targetFeature

`u8x16` gets x86's aesenc, aesenclast, aesdec, aesdeclast, aesimc and
aeskeygenassist(rcon), and ARM's aese, aesd and aesmc, with the checks
`u8x16.hasAesX86()`, `hasAesArm()` and `hasAes()`. Each method is the
instruction where the CPU has it, the other architecture's instructions
where those are what it has, and otherwise an exact emulation in Velt
(table based, for correctness only; std's AES keeps its constant-time
path when `hasAes()` is false).

Compiler: `Ext::AesX86`/`AesArm` and their `ExtOp`s in velt_vir's
registry, `targetFeature("aes-x86" | "aes-arm")` in sema, LLVM lowering to
`llvm.x86.aesni.*` and `llvm.aarch64.crypto.*`, zeros under Cranelift.
An AES target includes the carry-less multiplication of its architecture
(`Ext::covers`), so a gated AES-GCM loop is bare instructions for both,
and in any gated function the other architecture's checks fold to false.

No runtime flag (no new Rust in velt_rt, #2241): the check is true where
the build guarantees the extension (a gated function and its clones), and
otherwise std/simd/aes.vlt computes it once in Velt from the carry-less
flag plus `velt_simd_cpuid` (CPUID leaf 1, ECX bit 25; a new inline-asm
intrinsic, zeros off x86-64) on x86-64, and from the PMULL flag on arm64,
honoring `VELT_SIMD_DISABLE=aes`. `--cpu` does not make the check
constant, since the start-up check's feature lists are Rust.

Tested: golden std/simd_aes (FIPS-197 A.1 key expansion, Appendix B,
C.1 and C.3 vectors through both instruction sequences, encrypt and
decrypt, and every operation on 10,000 random inputs folded into one
checksum, so debug/emulation, release/instructions and
VELT_SIMD_DISABLE=aes agree; also run on wasm) and
std/simd_aeskeygenassist_range; veltc gated_functions (the gated AES
functions reach no check, CPUID or emulation, on x86-64 and arm64);
velt_opt gated_clones and velt_codegen_llvm unit tests; the x86-64 build
run under Rosetta in Docker (instructions and emulation give the same
checksum). Each test was seen failing on a deliberately broken version.
